{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.FluidDynamics.surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.Abs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.QIntegerCanonical"],"declarations":57,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.overlap_race_strict","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.overlap_race_strict","kind":"theorem","line":1152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L1152-L1166","doc":"**The race inequality.** If the overlap-crossing equation `(1−u)³ = x²`\nholds with `x ≠ 0` (positive core size), then `u < 1`: overlap engages\nstrictly before the singular time. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.riccati_lower_growth","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.riccati_lower_growth","kind":"theorem","line":1109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L1109-L1138","doc":"**Discrete Riccati lower growth.** `Ω_{n+1} ≥ Ω_n + δc'Ω_n²` with `δc' ≥ 0`\n⇒ the sequence is non-decreasing and `Ω_n ≥ Ω₀ + n·δc'Ω₀²`: linear growth\nat the initial production rate, forever. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.global_finiteness_subthreshold","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.global_finiteness_subthreshold","kind":"theorem","line":1080,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L1080-L1095","doc":"**Small-data global finiteness.** Sub-threshold finite data stays finite at\nevery step, globally. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.global_persistence_subthreshold","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.global_persistence_subthreshold","kind":"theorem","line":1054,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L1054-L1078","doc":"**Small-data global persistence.** With the damped low-mode step\n`Ω_{n+1} ≤ Ω_n + δ(cΩ_n² − νμΩ_n)` and sub-threshold data\n`c·Ω₀ ≤ ν·μ`, the enstrophy never increases: `Ω_n ≤ Ω₀` for **every** `n`.\nNo horizon, no `ε` hypothesis — the discrete in-QOU small-data global\nregularity statement. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.local_persistence","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.local_persistence","kind":"theorem","line":1018,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L1018-L1036","doc":"**Local persistence, finiteness form.** Under the bootstrap hypotheses with\nfinite initial enstrophy, the low-mode block is finite at every step of the\nhorizon — the in-QOU discrete local well-posedness statement, uniform in\n`ε`. Global continuation beyond the horizon is the (named, open) remainder\nof the epic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.local_bootstrap","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.local_bootstrap","kind":"theorem","line":972,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L972-L1016","doc":"**Discrete quadratic bootstrap (local persistence).** If the low-mode block\nobeys the quadratic step `Ω_{n+1} ≤ Ω_n + δcΩ_n²` and the horizon satisfies\n`N·δ·c·Ω₀ ≤ 1/4`, then `Ω_n ≤ 2Ω₀` for every `n ≤ N`. No hypothesis on `ε`:\nthe local theory is uniform in the substrate deformation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.split_persistence","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.split_persistence","kind":"theorem","line":938,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L938-L953","doc":"**Split persistence.** With a *finite* low-mode factor `G`, finite initial\nblocks, and the high modes damped, the total enstrophy is finite at every\nstep. Gevrey persistence thereby reduces to the finiteness of the low-mode\neffective rate — the open GP3 content. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.split_gronwall","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.split_gronwall","kind":"theorem","line":927,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L927-L936","doc":"**Split Gronwall.** Low-mode block growing at factor `G`, high-mode block\nnon-increasing (above the damping threshold): the total obeys\n`L n + H n ≤ G^n·L 0 + H 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.le_initial_of_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.le_initial_of_step","kind":"theorem","line":919,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L919-L925","doc":"A step-non-increasing sequence is bounded by its initial value. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.damping_threshold","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.damping_threshold","kind":"theorem","line":908,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L908-L917","doc":"**q-damping threshold.** Once a mode clears the threshold\n`Λ ≤ ν·[k]_q²`, its per-step factor is at most `1`: the mode is\nnon-growing. On the super-classical side `[k]_q` grows exponentially, so\nevery `Λ` is cleared at a finite mode index. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.gronwall_finite_horizon","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.gronwall_finite_horizon","kind":"theorem","line":876,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L876-L892","doc":"**Finite-horizon Gevrey-enstrophy persistence for finite rate.** If the\nper-step growth factor `G` is a *finite* surreal and the initial enstrophy\nis finite, the enstrophy stays finite at every fixed step `n`. The open\nwall (`p2-critical-exponent`): the naive `G = 1 + Λ_q δ` with\n`Λ_q ~ ε^{-1}` is NOT finite, so this theorem applies exactly when the\nq-damping improves the naive rate — that improvement is the Gevrey-\npersistence question, not decided here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.IsFinite.pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.IsFinite.pow","kind":"theorem","line":870,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L870-L874","doc":"Finites are closed under powers. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.discrete_gronwall","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.discrete_gronwall","kind":"theorem","line":858,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L858-L868","doc":"**Discrete Gronwall.** `Ω_{n+1} ≤ G·Ω_n` with `G ≥ 0`, non-negative\nstates ⇒ `Ω_n ≤ G^n·Ω_0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.closure_excludes_deep_cascade","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.closure_excludes_deep_cascade","kind":"theorem","line":827,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L827-L843","doc":"**The dissipation closure excludes grade-1-deep cascades** (scope (a),\nstructural incompatibility — the theorem form of probe Model A). Under the\nclosure `ε·Ω_tot = 2(1+ε)·D` with finite `D` and finite `ε`, the tower\ncannot reach grade-1 cascade depth: the closure and a sustained\nKolmogorov-type cascade are mutually exclusive ingredients. Which one the\nQOU's actual flow realises is the open content (`p2-critical-exponent`);\nneither is asserted. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.deep_cascade_not_grade_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.deep_cascade_not_grade_one","kind":"theorem","line":807,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L807-L825","doc":"**Grade-1-deep cascades beat the grade-1 cap.** If `0 ≤ ε`, amplitudes are\nnon-negative, the total dominates the truncations, and the tower reaches\ngrade-1 cascade depth (`hcascade`), then `Ω_tot` is **not** finite of\nω-grade 1. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.gevrey_grade_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.gevrey_grade_zero","kind":"theorem","line":727,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L727-L792","doc":"**Gevrey amplitudes are grade-0 regular (theorem form of probe Model B).**\nIf the amplitudes decay geometrically, `0 ≤ a_k ≤ C·r^k` with `C : ℕ`, and\nthe combined damping `q² r ≤ 1/2` holds (`1 ≤ q`, `0 ≤ r` — satisfied at\n`q = 1 + ε`, `ε` infinitesimal, for any fixed `r < 1/2`), then every\ntruncation obeys the uniform bound `Ω_M(q) ≤ 6C`, and with the lub property\nthe total surreal enstrophy is **finite**: the QOU-internal flow with fixed\nGevrey data sits in the grade-0 **regular** branch of `grade_trichotomy`.\nIn-QOU, blow-up is thereby confined to non-Gevrey (sustained/forced)\nspectra. Whether the internal dynamics *preserves* the Gevrey class as\n`q → 1⁺` (Gevrey persistence, the analytic core of\n`thm:q-ns-wellposedness`) is the remaining open content; not decided\nhere. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.sum_sq_half_pow_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.sum_sq_half_pow_le","kind":"theorem","line":719,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L719-L725","doc":"**The half-geometric quadratic sum is bounded by `6`.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.sum_sq_half_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.sum_sq_half_pow","kind":"theorem","line":707,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L707-L717","doc":"**Closed form for the half-geometric quadratic sum:**\n`Σ_{k<M} k²(1/2)^k = 6 − (2M² + 4M + 6)(1/2)^M`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_le_mul_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_le_mul_pow","kind":"theorem","line":699,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L699-L705","doc":"`[k]_q ≤ k·q^k` for `1 ≤ q` (each of the `k` powers is at most `q^k`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.grade_trichotomy","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.grade_trichotomy","kind":"theorem","line":658,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L658-L686","doc":"**The grade trichotomy.** For infinitesimal `ε`, the enstrophy observable\nfalls into exactly one of three mutually-exclusive regimes (disjoint by\nconstruction: the first is finite, the last two are not; the last two\ndiffer on the criterion):\n\n* **grade 0 (regular):** `Ω` finite — and then `ε·Ω` is automatically\n  infinitesimal;\n* **saturated (`α = 1`):** `Ω` infinite with `ε·Ω` non-infinitesimal — the\n  flow saturates the dissipation-closure cap;\n* **intermediate (`0 < α < 1`):** `Ω` infinite yet `ε·Ω` still\n  infinitesimal.\n\nHence the entire remaining α question of the super-classical side reduces\nto the single surreal criterion **\"is `ε · Ω(1+ε)` infinitesimal?\"** —\ninfinitesimal + finite `Ω` ⟺ regular; non-infinitesimal ⟺ saturated\nblow-up (`saturated_implies_infinite`). Which regime the QOU's flow\nrealises is the open critical-exponent content (`p2-critical-exponent`);\nnot decided here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.saturated_implies_infinite","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.saturated_implies_infinite","kind":"theorem","line":649,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L649-L656","doc":"**Saturation forces blow-up.** If `ε · Ω` is *not* infinitesimal (the flow\nsaturates the grade-1 cap, `α = 1`), then `Ω` is not a finite surreal —\nthe QOU-internal q-NS observable blows up. Contrapositive of\n`Infinitesimal.mul_finite`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.enstrophy_grade_one_of_dissipation_closure","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.enstrophy_grade_one_of_dissipation_closure","kind":"theorem","line":617,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L617-L638","doc":"**Grade cap from the dissipation closure.** Under the dissipation closure\n`ε · Ω = 2(1+ε) · D` (the `q = 1+ε` reading of\n`enstrophy_alpha_one_of_dissipation_closure` with `ν_q · Ω = D` cleared of\ndenominators) with finite mean dissipation `D` and finite `ε`, the\nsuper-classical enstrophy is **finite of ω-grade 1**: `ε · Ω` is finite, so\n`Ω` blows up at most like `ε⁻¹` — the surreal statement of the critical\nexponent `α = 1`. The remaining open content of the super-classical side is\nthereby the gap **grade 1 vs grade 0**: whether the flow saturates the\nclosure's `ε⁻¹` cap (blow-up, `α = 1`) or stays at grade 0 (regular) — the\n`α` question of `p2-critical-exponent`, not decided here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.FiniteOfGrade","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.FiniteOfGrade","kind":"def","line":611,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L611-L615","doc":"`x` is **finite of ω-grade `n`** (relative to the infinitesimal `ε`):\n`ε^n · x` is finite. Grade `0` is ordinary finiteness; grade `1` allows one\nfactor of the infinite surreal `ε⁻¹` — the surreal bookkeeping of the\nblow-up order `ord_ω` (`def:surreal-blowup-calculus`, §7ab). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.superclassical_infinite_of_classical_unbounded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.superclassical_infinite_of_classical_unbounded","kind":"theorem","line":594,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L594-L609","doc":"**Forward direction on the super-classical side.** If the total surreal\nenstrophy dominates every truncation and the classical enstrophy is\nunbounded, the surreal observable at `q = 1 + ε` is infinite — immediate\nfrom the reversed domination; no infinitesimality of `ε` needed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.enstrophyN_ge_classical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.enstrophyN_ge_classical","kind":"theorem","line":582,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L582-L592","doc":"**Enstrophy domination, reversed:** with non-negative amplitudes the\nsuper-classical enstrophy dominates the classical one at every truncation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_ge_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_ge_self","kind":"theorem","line":572,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L572-L580","doc":"**Termwise classical domination, reversed:** for `0 ≤ ε`,\n`k ≤ [k]_{1+ε}` — each super-classical q-integer is at least its classical\nvalue (every power `(1+ε)^j ≥ 1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.surreal_finite_of_classical_bounded_subclassical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.surreal_finite_of_classical_bounded_subclassical","kind":"theorem","line":527,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L527-L558","doc":"**Sub-classical closure of the converse — NO spectral hypothesis.** On the\n`q = 1 − ε` side (`0 ≤ ε ≤ 1` — in particular any positive infinitesimal,\nwhich sits in the golden window by `golden_window_mem`), bounded classical\nenstrophy plus the definitional lub property already force the surreal\nobservable to be finite: by termwise domination every truncation satisfies\n`Ω_M(1−ε) ≤ Ω_M(1) < B`, so `Ω_tot ≤ B`. **No tail hypothesis, no\namplitude-finiteness, not even infinitesimality of `ε` is needed.**\n\nTogether with `classical_unbounded_implies_surreal_infinite` (whose\ndomination hypothesis is the definitional lower-limit property), the bridge\nbiconditional `classical_regular_iff_finite` **closes on the sub-classical\napproach**, given only the definitional limit pair (truncations below\n`Ω_tot`, `Ω_tot` a lub) and non-negative amplitudes. The honest remaining\nopen content of the whole bridge is now confined to the **super-classical\napproach** `q = 1 + ε` (`[k]_{1+ε} ≥ k`), where the tail question is\ngenuine. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.enstrophyN_le_classical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.enstrophyN_le_classical","kind":"theorem","line":514,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L514-L525","doc":"**Enstrophy domination:** with non-negative amplitudes, the sub-classical\nenstrophy is dominated by the classical enstrophy at every truncation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_le_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_le_self","kind":"theorem","line":503,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L503-L512","doc":"**Termwise classical domination:** for `0 ≤ ε ≤ 1`, `[k]_{1−ε} ≤ k` — each\nsub-classical q-integer is at most its classical value (every power\n`(1−ε)^j ≤ 1`). The exact opposite of the super-classical side. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.surreal_finite_of_uniform_tail","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.surreal_finite_of_uniform_tail","kind":"theorem","line":473,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L473-L489","doc":"**Combined reduction.** Given a QOU's surreal enstrophy data with\nnon-negative finite amplitudes, the lub property, a uniform tail bound, and\nbounded classical enstrophy, the surreal observable is finite — the full\nconverse half assembled from `uv_exhaustion_of_uniform_tail` +\n`surreal_finite_of_classical_bounded`. The open remainder of the whole\nbridge is now the single **uniform tail bound** hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.uv_exhaustion_of_uniform_tail","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.uv_exhaustion_of_uniform_tail","kind":"theorem","line":443,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L443-L471","doc":"**UV exhaustion from a uniform tail bound.** If the truncations are\nmonotone (non-negative amplitudes), `Ω_tot` is a least-upper-bound-type\nlimit of the truncations (`hlub`), and the tower satisfies a **uniform tail\nbound** past some level `N` (`htail`: every deeper truncation exceeds\n`Ω_N(1−ε)` by at most a finite `c` — the surreal `N_eff` cutoff physics,\ncf. the `k_max ≈ 4` active-mode truncation of `prop:leray-profile`), then\nthe **UV-exhaustion hypothesis of `surreal_finite_of_classical_bounded`\nholds**. Combined with that theorem and\n`classical_unbounded_implies_surreal_infinite`, the bridge biconditional is\nproved modulo the uniform tail bound alone — which is exactly where the\nuniform damping `[k]_{1−ε} ≤ ε⁻¹` (`qInt_le_inv`) gives the tail sum\n`Σ_{k≥N} [k]² a_k ≤ ε⁻² Σ_{k≥N} a_k` a chance to be finite that the\nclassical point `q = 1` does not have. Whether the QOU's amplitude tail\nmass is `O(ε²)` past `N_eff` remains the open spectral question; it is not\nasserted. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.enstrophyN_mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.enstrophyN_mono","kind":"theorem","line":433,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L433-L441","doc":"Monotonicity of the truncated enstrophy in the cutoff (non-negative\namplitudes): more modes, more enstrophy. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_le_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_le_inv","kind":"theorem","line":413,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L413-L429","doc":"**Uniform UV damping.** On the `q = 1 − ε < 1` side the q-integers are\nbounded *uniformly in the mode index*: `[k]_{1−ε} ≤ ε⁻¹` for every `k`\n(an infinite surreal bound, but `k`-independent) — in sharp contrast to\n`[k]_1 = k`, which is unbounded. This is the algebraic seed of UV\nexhaustion: at the infinitesimally-perturbed substrate the spectral tower\nretains one-sided UV control that the classical point `q = 1` has lost\n(`conj:regularity-breakdown` Obstruction 1). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.eps_mul_qInt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.eps_mul_qInt","kind":"theorem","line":401,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L401-L411","doc":"**Exact geometric identity:** `ε · [k]_{1−ε} = 1 − (1−ε)^k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.golden_window_mem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.golden_window_mem","kind":"theorem","line":376,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L376-L397","doc":"**Golden-window membership.** For every positive infinitesimal `ε` the\nsurreal substrate point `q = 1 − ε` lies strictly inside the classical band\n`q_gold < q < 1` (`q_gold = s²`, `s` the positive root of `s² + s = 1`,\ni.e. `s = 1/φ`): the elliptic fiber is smooth and the confinement window's\nhypotheses apply at the infinitesimally-perturbed substrate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.surreal_finite_of_classical_bounded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.surreal_finite_of_classical_bounded","kind":"theorem","line":327,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L327-L364","doc":"**The converse half of the UV wall, under UV exhaustion.** Suppose the total\nsurreal enstrophy `Ω_tot` is non-negative, is **UV-exhausted** by the tower\n(`hexh`: some finite truncation captures it up to a finite constant `c` —\n\"no enstrophy at infinite mode index beyond finite excess\"), and the\n**classical** enstrophy is bounded along the tower (`hbnd` — classical\nregularity read at `q = 1`). Then `Ω_tot` **is** a finite surreal.\n\nTogether with `classical_unbounded_implies_surreal_infinite` this proves the\nbridge biconditional `classical_regular_iff_finite`\n(`qou_ns_surreal_bridge`) **modulo one named structural hypothesis: UV\nexhaustion.** The failure mode exhaustion excludes is exactly the\n\"conspiracy\" scenario — enstrophy concentrating at infinite surreal mode\nindex `k ~ ω` (sub-classical scales) while every finite truncation stays\nbounded. Whether the QOU-internal flow satisfies UV exhaustion is the open\nanalytic content; it is **not** proved here.\n\nProof: `Ω_tot ≤ Ω_N(1−ε) + c`, and by the finite-mode theorem\n`Ω_N(1−ε) < Ω_N(1) + 1 < B + 1`, so `0 ≤ Ω_tot < B + 1 + c`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.classical_unbounded_implies_surreal_infinite","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.classical_unbounded_implies_surreal_infinite","kind":"theorem","line":285,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L285-L323","doc":"**Half of the UV wall, proved.** Suppose the total surreal enstrophy\n`Ω_tot` dominates every finite truncation (`hdom` — the monotone-limit /\ntower-domination property, automatic for non-negative mode amplitudes), and\nthe **classical** enstrophy is unbounded along the tower (`hunb` — classical\nenstrophy blow-up, `conj:regularity-breakdown` item 1 read at `q = 1`).\nThen `Ω_tot` is **not** a finite surreal: the surreal observable is\ninfinite.\n\nThis proves one direction of the open bridge biconditional\n`classical_regular_iff_finite` (`qou_ns_surreal_bridge`), in contrapositive\nform: **surreal-finite ⇒ classically bounded**. The *converse* — that a\nsurreal-infinite observable forces classical divergence, i.e. that the\ninfinite part cannot come solely from infinitesimal-scale (sub-classical)\ncontributions — remains the open analytic content, and is deliberately not\nasserted. The wall is thereby **halved**, not closed.\n\nProof: from `|Ω_tot| < m`, pick `N` with `Ω_N(1) > m + 1`; by the\nfinite-mode theorem `Ω_N(1−ε)` is within an infinitesimal (hence within\n`1`) of `Ω_N(1)`, so `Ω_tot ≥ Ω_N(1−ε) > m ≥ Ω_tot` — contradiction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.enstrophy_perturbation_infinitesimal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.enstrophy_perturbation_infinitesimal","kind":"theorem","line":245,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L245-L281","doc":"**S3 (capstone).** At every finite truncation `N`, the surreal enstrophy at\n`q = 1 − ε` deviates from the classical enstrophy `Ω_N(1) = Σ k² a_k` by an\ninfinitesimal — i.e. the **standard part of the finite-mode surreal\nenstrophy is the classical enstrophy**. Requires only that each mode\namplitude is finite.\n\nThis proves the finite-mode half of the surreal bridge\n(`qou_ns_surreal_bridge.blowup_iff_infinite` restricted to any fixed\ntruncation) and localises the open wall in the `N → ∞` (UV) limit: as\n`q → 1⁺` the active-mode count `N_eff ~ log_q Re` diverges, and whether the\nstandard part commutes with that limit is precisely the open\n`classical_regular_iff_finite` hypothesis — NOT proved here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.enstrophyN","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.enstrophyN","kind":"def","line":238,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L238-L243","doc":"The **finite-mode enstrophy** at substrate value `q`, truncation `N`, mode\namplitudes `a`: `Ω_N(q) = Σ_{k<N} [k]_q² · a k` (the enstrophy order\nparameter of `conj:regularity-breakdown` / `p2-critical-exponent`,\ntruncated at `N` modes). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_one_sub_infinitesimal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_one_sub_infinitesimal","kind":"theorem","line":224,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L224-L234","doc":"**S2.** `[k]_{1−ε} − k` is infinitesimal for every fixed `k`: at an\ninfinitesimally-perturbed substrate the q-integer eigenvalues sit\ninfinitesimally close to their classical values. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.pow_one_sub_infinitesimal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.pow_one_sub_infinitesimal","kind":"theorem","line":207,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L207-L222","doc":"Powers of `1 − ε` deviate from `1` by an infinitesimal (fixed exponent):\n`(1−ε)^j − 1` is infinitesimal. Induction via\n`a^(j+1) − 1 = (a^j − 1)·a − ε` with `a = 1 − ε`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_one","kind":"theorem","line":203,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L203-L205","doc":"`[k]_1 = k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_eq_canonical_geo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt_eq_canonical_geo","kind":"theorem","line":195,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L195-L201","doc":"**Bridge to the canonical geometric q-integer.**  This site is the\ngeometric form `∑_{j<k} q^j`, not the balanced one — so it ties to `qIntGeo`,\nand tying it to `qIntBal` would be exactly the mis-consolidation\n`QIntegerCanonical` warns about.  Unconditional: `qIntGeo` asks only for a\n`CommRing`, which `K` has as a `Field`, and neither side divides. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.qInt","kind":"def","line":190,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L190-L193","doc":"The **q-integer** `[k]_q = Σ_{j<k} q^j` (so `[k]_1 = k`): the spectral\neigenvalue datum of the q-NS tower whose `q → 1` degeneration to `k` is\nObstruction 1 of `conj:regularity-breakdown`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.infinitesimal_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.infinitesimal_sum","kind":"theorem","line":176,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L176-L186","doc":"A finite sum of infinitesimals is infinitesimal. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.IsFinite.mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.IsFinite.mul","kind":"theorem","line":160,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L160-L174","doc":"Finites are closed under multiplication. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.IsFinite.neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.IsFinite.neg","kind":"theorem","line":156,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L156-L158","doc":"Finites are closed under negation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.IsFinite.add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.IsFinite.add","kind":"theorem","line":144,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L144-L154","doc":"Finites are closed under addition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.Infinitesimal.isFinite","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.Infinitesimal.isFinite","kind":"theorem","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L138-L142","doc":"Every infinitesimal is finite. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.isFinite_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.isFinite_one","kind":"theorem","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L134-L136","doc":"`1` is finite. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.isFinite_natCast","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.isFinite_natCast","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L128-L132","doc":"Natural-number constants are finite. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.Infinitesimal.mul_finite","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.Infinitesimal.mul_finite","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L109-L126","doc":"An infinitesimal times a finite element is infinitesimal. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.Infinitesimal.add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.Infinitesimal.add","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L87-L107","doc":"Closure of infinitesimals under addition (via the `2n` trick). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.Infinitesimal.neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.Infinitesimal.neg","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L84-L85","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.infinitesimal_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.infinitesimal_zero","kind":"theorem","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L81-L82","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.IsFinite","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.IsFinite","kind":"def","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L78-L79","doc":"`x` is **finite** (bounded by a natural): `|x| < n` for some `n : ℕ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.Infinitesimal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.Infinitesimal","kind":"def","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/FluidDynamics/surreal_enstrophy_finite_modes.lean#L73-L76","doc":"`x` is **infinitesimal** (relative to the naturals): every natural multiple\nof `|x|` stays below `1`. On an archimedean field this forces `x = 0`; on a\nsurreal line it carries the genuine infinitesimals (e.g. `ε = 1/ω`). ","signatureChunk":0}]}